From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0243] clock: read the NPU vote bits before writing one

C2-06 is the first card whose object is a vote rather than a clock. A voted
branch in the vendor tree is not enabled by writing its enable bit; the bit is a
ballot, and the branch only runs while at least one ballot is in the box. The
release path is asymmetric in a way that matters later: enable polls the halt
status for up to 500 us, disable waits 10 us, returns 0 and never concludes that
the branch stopped, because another master may still be voting.

Writing a ballot is therefore not a state change this driver can verify from its
own write, and the card that does it has to be designed against what the device
already holds. That is what this patch adds: the observation, not the vote.

sweet,clock-vote-probe (4 bytes, its own opt-in, arms nothing else) maps one more
page - vendor offset 0x0014d000, the NPU block; physical 0x0014d000 - and reads
four branch control registers once:

    0x004  gcc_npu_cfg_ahb_clk    plain halt branch, the page's control
    0x008  gcc_npu_axi_clk        BRANCH_VOTED
    0x00c  gcc_npu_trig_clk       BRANCH_VOTED
    0x010  gcc_npu_at_clk         BRANCH_VOTED

The fifth range is appended after the four mandatory ones, so the ADT reg order
is still the window ABI and index 4 is the optional page. It is refused with the
same four details the mandatory windows use (window-absent, address-mismatch,
length-short, no-virtual-base) because the resource name already says which page
is wrong. writeRegister() keeps refusing index 4: this pass is read-only, and the
vote card will widen it with its own guard and its own precondition.

Each reading is one row, and the summary row states the arithmetic rather than a
verdict:

    phase=probe ... name= voted= enable= clk-off= value=
    phase=probe-sample voted=3 enable-set= enable-clear= clk-off= failed= reads= writes=

An enable bit already set at boot is a vote this driver did not place. That is a
result, not a failure - it is the second holder the aggregate-stays-on half of
the next card needs, and it cannot be produced on demand - so the probe reports
it and succeeds. Only a failed read fails the pass: then the page is not what the
card assumes, the ERROR rows say which register, and the start summary carries
detail=probe-incomplete. Nothing here writes, so no ring can be damaged by
pulling the switch on the wrong tree.

Backward compatibility is the same rule as C2-03/04/05: an image without the
property maps four windows, reads nineteen registers and prints its start row
byte-identically to 0136. The property is additive and exactly removable - the
generator puts the range and the property behind one flag, and the ADT test
proves that dropping both reproduces the tree without it.

